{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373410838,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c8fd5ecc001yUnHU3I4x5vXOg","messageID":"msg_1c8fd4d12001exnukdxHV2iIN1","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>Now let me look at the frontend.\n\nNote that the lock file is installed with `-c` constraints, so adding new dependencies would require updating the lock file. The task says the sandbox can install packages from public package registries. Let me check what's available. For markdown rendering, I'd like `markdown` or `markdown-it-py` package. But adding to requirements.txt means the lock file needs updating. Actually, the Dockerfile installs `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`, so if a package is in requirements.txt but not in the lock file, pip will fail because constraints don't include it... Actually no — constraints only constrain versions of packages that are listed. If a package isn't in constraints, pip will install the pinned version from requirements.txt without constraint. That works fine.\n\nWait, but actually `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`: constraints file entries are only applied to packages already in the requirements. A package in requirements.txt but not in constraints will be installed at the specified version. Yes that works.\n\nBut I should update the lock file too as the README says. Let me first check the frontend files.\n\nAlso, for markdown rendering, I could write my own minimal Markdown renderer in Python to avoid dependency issues. But using a well-known library like `markdown` (Python-Markdown) or `markdown-it-py` is better quality. Let me check if pip install works in the sandbox.\n\nLet me lookat the frontend first.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373410508,"end":1794373410696}}}
