{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794373851837,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9041aa2001RIsrNd1Dv36Xh4","messageID":"msg_1c9039855001OXyDHh4gtvSKw4","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373851837,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9041aa6001JqHlRbr4TL7cJa","messageID":"msg_1c9039855001OXyDHh4gtvSKw4","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>Interesting — the constraint file is being used with `-c` but pip turns on require-hashes because hashes exist in the constraints file? Actually the error says hashes are required. Hmm, pip's `-c` with hashes in constraint file triggers `--require-hashes` mode. But the Dockerfile does exactly this:\n\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\n\nWait — but the original lock file exists and the Dockerfile works with it presumably. Let me check: the original requirements.lock has hashes for all packages including flask. And requirements.txt lists flask==3.1.3 without hashes. So the same error would occur... unless pip only enforces this when the constraint's hashes are for the exact requirement. Hmm, actually pip's require-hashes mode gets enabled when any requirement in the *requirements file* has a hash. For constraints, pip checks...\n\nActually the error message lists flask==3.1.3 --hash=... so it's telling me the hash that matched. The issue is that in require-hashes mode, all requirements need hashes. The requirements.txt has no hashes. But the original Dockerfile worked? Let me test with the original lock file to see.\n\nLet me check withgit stash.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373851814,"end":1794373851833}}}
