{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373866756,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c90454f10011HtGC5x3Ptwe5J","messageID":"msg_1c9041dbd001JbqPmLdDky46Gg","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>Interesting — the original setup has the same behavior. The issue is that the mirror's flask 3.1.3 wheel hash differs from the lock (the mirror may serve a different build). Actually the lock lists two hashes for flask, and pip found `f4bcbefc...` which IS in the lock. Hmm, but the error says hashes are missing from some requirements — that's because `--require-hashes` mode is auto-enabled when the *constraint* file has hashes? Actually no: constraints files don't trigger require-hashes mode... but pip does check: \"If any package has a hash\" — this is because the constraints file's hash entries... Hmm.\n\nActually the real issue: pip's `-c` with hashes enables require-hashes mode for the requirements. The error mentions flask==3.1.3 with the hash f4bcbefc... which IS listed in the lock. So pip found the right hash, but it wants hashes on the requirements file lines themselves.\n\nWait — but the Dockerfile does exactly `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`. So in the real Docker build (against real PyPI), would this work? The original lock's flask hashes are the real PyPI ones. The mirror here serves a different flask build? No — it says the downloaded archive had hash f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c, which matches the lock. So the hash matches; the problem is pip requires hashes on the *requirement lines* in requirements.txt when require-hashes mode is active.\n\nHmm, but the same failure occurs with the original unmodified files, which presumably worked in the Docker build. The difference: in Docker, the index is real PyPI, and pip's require-hashes auto-enable happens when hashes appear in the requirements file... For constraints files, pip docs say: \"constraints files may not contain hashes\"? Actually pip's `--require-hashes` is triggered by hashes in the *requirements* file. Let me check: pip's `_check_binary_allowed`... In pip source, `RequirementSet` checks `self.require_hashes` which comes from `cmd_opts.require_hashes` — and in `pip._internal.cli.req_command`, there's:\n\n```python\nif self.require_hashes and not self.options.ignore_installed ...\n```\nand in `pip._internal.req.req_file`:\n```python\nif options.require_hashes ...\n```\nAlso in `pip._internal.cli.cmdoptions`, `--require-hashes` is a general option.\n\nThere's logic in `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n```python\n    def _get_requirements(...)\n```\nHmm, and there's:\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def check_requirements(...)\n```\n\nActually there IS logic in pip: in `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py`, when parsing a constraints file, hashes are handled via `handle_hash_options`. And in `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n\n```python\n        if self.options.require_hashes:\n            ...\n```\n\nAlso `pip/_internal/operations/prepare.py`...\n\nHmm, the key bit: `pip._internal.cli.req_command`:\n```python\n    def handle_pip_version_check...\n```\n\nLet me just look at the actual pip source installed to determine whether constraints hashes trigger require-hashes mode. Actually, from the error: \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements.\" and it lists flask==3.1.3 with the hash it downloaded. This is the `InstallationError` raised in `pip/_internal/req/req_install.py`:\n\n```python\n    def check_if_exists...\n```\nActually it's in `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/candidates.py`? No — `pip/_internal/wheel_builder.py`:\n```python\n        if req.is_wheel_from_cache...\n```\nHmm, it's in `pip/_internal/req/req_install.py`:\n```python\n    def assert_source_matches_version...\n```\n\nThe message \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements.\" comes from `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def _check_hashes(reqs):\n```\n\nActually it's `pip/_internal/utils/hashes.py` → `Hashes.check_against_chunks`. The \"missing from some requirements\" wording comes from `pip/_internal/req/req_install.py::check_hashes`? Let me just search the installed pip.\n\nThe important thing: the *original* repo has the same behavior in this sandbox, meaning the Docker build in the grading environment might work differently (real PyPI). But actually the error is about hash *mode*, not mismatch. Let me investigate: pip auto-enables require-hashes when a requirements file contains hashes. The `-c` constraints file — pip's `req_file.py` `handle_line` for constraints... Let me check whether constraints' hashes get merged into the requirements' `hash_options`.\n\nIn pip's `req_file.py`:\n```python\n    def handle_line(...):\n        ...\n        if reqs:\n            ...\n```\nand\n```python\nclass RequirementsFileParser:\n    def __init__(...):\n```\nThere's:\n```python\n        # in ParsedRequirement\n        self.options = options\n```\nAnd in `pip/_internal/req/constructors.py` `install_req_from_parsed_requirement`.\n\nIn `pip/_internal/cli/req_command.py`:\n```python\n    def _get_pip_version...\n```\n\nHmm. Let's look at `pip/_internal/commands/install.py`:\n```python\n    def get_requirements(...)\n```\n\nHonestly, the simplest explanation: pip's `Options` sets `require_hashes=True` when `finder` sees hashes in *any* parsed requirement including constraints? The constraint file hashes are attached to the requirement objects created from constraints... and `install_req_from_line` with hashes → `req.hash_options`. In `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/factory.py` `Factory._make_candidate_from_link`... The check for missing hashes happens in `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/factory.py`:\n\n```python\n    def _check_skip_reason(...)\n```\nHmm, actually: `pip/_internal/operations/prepare.py`:\n```python\n        if not self.require_hashes and ...:\n```\nLet me just check the source directly. But more important: does the Docker build work? In Docker, pip installs from real PyPI with `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`. If pip auto-enables require-hashes due to constraint hashes, the build would fail with the original repo too — and the original repo is stated to be a working baseline. So probably in the Docker build it works... but the same pip version is used (pip in python:3.13-slim). The only difference is the index.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. In the sandbox the error says flask's downloaded archive had hash f4bcbefc... which IS in the lock file. So the hash matched. The failure is that requirements.txt lines have no hashes. In Docker with real PyPI, the same thing should happen... unless the downloaded flask from PyPI has hash 0ef0e52b... which is also in the lock.\n\nHmm, so why would it work in Docker? Let me actually check pip's logic. I'll grep the pip source.\n\nActually — maybe the issue is that the sandbox's pip is 25.0.1 while Docker's python:3.13-slim pip is newer (25.x too). Let me just check pip's source for where require_hashes is auto-enabled.\n\nLet me look for \"require_hashes\" in pip source.</think>\n\nLet me check why the hash mode triggers, since the baseline has the same structure:","time":{"start":1794373866737,"end":1794373866752}}}
