{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373885631,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9049ea3001Mf6PxO0QZHp7r2","messageID":"msg_1c90480610017aT9QHU7SSgJ6Q","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The problem is the constraints file has hashes. When pip uses `-c` with hashes, it inherits hash requirements. Let me check how pip handles constraints with hashes... The error says \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\". Wait, that hash is in the lock file. Hmm, but the error says \"Hashes are missing from some requirements\" for flask==3.1.3. Wait it lists the hash it actually downloaded. So the downloaded hash of flask 3.1.3 is `f4bcbefc...` which IS in the lockfile. The problem: requirements.txt lists `flask==3.1.3` with no hash, but the constraints file has hashes. Hmm — actually with `-c` constraints, the hashes from the constraint file get merged into the requirement, and then `--require-hashes` mode is triggered by... hmm.\n\nWait, the baseline (git stash) also failed with the same error! So this is a pre-existing condition in the environment: `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` triggers require-hashes mode because the constraint entries have hashes. Interesting — so the Docker build itself would fail the same way?\n\nActually let's check: in the Docker build, `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`. pip's `cmd_options` has `require_hashes`. Looking at the code path: `req_command.py:286: options.require_hashes = True` is in `RequirementsCommandMixin.handle_pip_version_check`? Let me look more carefully — that check is `if any(req.has_hash_options for req in requirements)`. requirements comes from parsing `-r` files only, not constraints. Hmm but the error occurs.\n\nLet me look at how the hashes get attached. In factory.py line 460 `constraint.hashes`. Constraints with hashes → the ireq gets the hash → require_hashes mode.\n\nActually pip docs: \"--require-hashes\" is implied when any package has a hash in the requirements file. For constraints, pip has `constraint.hashes` merged. Let's look at pip source line 250-320 of factory.py.\n\nThe key question: does the Docker build work at baseline? The Dockerfile uses the same command. So if it fails here it would fail there too... unless the difference is the pip version in the Docker image (python:3.13-slim ships pip 25.x) versus local pip 25.0.1.\n\nHmm, this matters. Let me check pip's handling more carefully. In factory.py:\n\n```python\n    def _make_candidate_from_link(...)\n```\n\nLet me look at where constraint.hashes is used and how require_hashes is derived.\n\nActually, in newer pip versions, there's this in `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/factory.py`:\n```python\n        if self._requires_hash ...\n```\n\nLet me check the actual source. But more practically: the baseline image presumably builds fine (the task says the workspace holds a minimal project for this stack). Let me test whether pip installs successfully with the baseline using a different approach — maybe I need `--no-deps`? No.\n\nLet me actually check: is the error occurring because of my local pip resolving differently? The error message says \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc...\" — that IS one of the hashes in the lock file for flask. Wait, the lock has flask hashes: 0ef0e52b... and f4bcbefc.... So pip downloaded the sdist/wheel and got f4bcbefc which is in the lock. So the hash matched, yet it complains that hashes are missing from some requirements — meaning requirement `flask==3.1.3` (from requirements.txt, no hash) needs a hash because require_hashes mode is on.\n\nSo the mode got enabled. Where? `options.require_hashes = True` at req_command.py:286 — that's inside `handle_pip_version_check`? Let me view the surrounding function.\n\nLet me check whether pip's Constraint objects carry hashes and whether pip merges them into ireq.hashes. In pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/factory.py:\n\n```python\n    def _make_candidate_from_link(...)-> InstallationCandidate:\n```\nand\n```python\n    def _make_requirement_from_install_req(self, ireq)\n```\n\nLet me look at factory.py:440-470.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373885603,"end":1794373885625}}}
