{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794373893160,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c904bc02001vkR2lh8NpIaGjk","messageID":"msg_1c9049f1a001WUxiUm9v19IzvX","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373893161,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c904bc06001cHIezKEh16291S","messageID":"msg_1c9049f1a001WUxiUm9v19IzvX","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The issue: the constraints file has hashes, which causes pip to require hashes for the requirements too. Wait — the constraint file's hashes are applied to the candidate constraint. Actually the error says \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc...\" meaning the constraint hash was found but the requirement from requirements.txt had no hash. Hmm, actually pip applies the constraint's hashes.\n\nWait — this error occurs with the ORIGINAL requirements.lock too (I tested with git stash). So it's a pre-existing environment issue: the pip version here is 25.0.1 and the container's pip is different. Let me check whether the Dockerfile's pip install would work. In the Docker image, python:3.13-slim has a newer pip. Let me check if newer pip handles `-c` with hashes differently.\n\nActually, the constraint file has hashes and pip merges constraint hashes into the requirement when require_hashes mode is on. The require_hashes mode gets enabled when *any requirement* has hash options — which includes constraints? Let's check `parse_requirements` for constraints.\n\nLooking at req_command.py around line 210-240, there's a section for constraints. Let me check if `has_hash_options` is checked for constraints too.\n\nActually the key question: does the Docker build work? The baseline Dockerfile + lock file is given as \"minimal project for this stack\", so presumably it builds. The environment here has pip 25.0.1 installed at /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages, but the Docker image python:3.13-slim has its own pip (likely 25.x too).\n\nHmm, but wait: pip 25.0.1 with `--require-hashes` triggered by constraints... Let me test with a newer pip in a venv to see behavior.\n\nActually, let me look more carefully. In pip, constraint files with hashes: pip's `parse_requirements` for constraints uses `install_req_from_parsed_requirement` with `user_supplied=False`. Then the `has_hash_options` check at line 286 only applies to `requirements` (from -r files and args), not constraints. So the require_hashes wouldn't be enabled by the constraint alone.\n\nBut the error message says hashes are missing for flask==3.1.3, which is in requirements.txt without hashes. So require_hashes WAS enabled. Hmm... maybe pip enables it because `options.require_hashes` gets set elsewhere. Let me check pip's newer versions... In pip 25.x, `Constraint` objects with hashes: `pip/_internal/req/constructors.py`.\n\nActually let me just check: maybe pip in the constraint parse path also sets require_hashes. Let's grep.\n\nAlternatively — the real question is whether the Docker build works. Since I can't run Docker here, I should be careful: my job is to keep the Dockerfile working as given. The baseline lock file was presumably generated the same way (pip-compile --generate-hashes) and the baseline Dockerfile does `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`. If that fails with pip 25.0.1 locally, it would presumably fail in the container too — unless the container's pip differs.\n\nLet me test in a fresh venv with pip from the index to see the newest pip behavior.\n\nActually, simpler: let me check whether pip's constraint handling adds hashes to requirements. Let me look at how constraints are applied.\n\nIn pip's resolver, `factory._iter_found_candidates(ireqs, specifier, hashes, ...)` where hashes comes from `constraint.hashes` — the Constraint object is created from constraint files. And `constraint` is built in `_make_requirement_from_install_req`... Let me look at how Constraint gets hashes.\n\nLet me check `pip/_internal/resolution/resolvelib/base.py` Constraint class.\n\nThe real fix regardless: pip in hash-checking mode requires that the *installed* requirement have hashes. When require_hashes is enabled and a constraint provides hashes, pip's `check_if_exists` uses `req.hashes(trust_internet=...)`.\n\nLet me just test: does `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` work with a newer pip? Let me install latest pip in a venv and try.\n\nActually, wait. Let me check whether pip enables require_hashes from constraints. In pip 25.0.1's `req_command.py` line ~286: `if any(req.has_hash_options for req in requirements)`. But `requirements` there only includes -r files and args, not constraints. Hmm, but constraints are parsed and appended to `options.constraints` separately.\n\nLet me look for `require_hashes` in the constraints parsing path.\n\nLet me just empiricallytest with newer pip.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373893126,"end":1794373893156}}}
