{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794373905328,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c904eb8f001SOD9rTDgX2P36y","messageID":"msg_1c904cf2c001av8Z1T48zTqijE","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373905328,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c904eb92001SwsQW7RAoGNuhO","messageID":"msg_1c904cf2c001av8Z1T48zTqijE","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The `-c constraints` file with hashes causes the requirement to have hash options in newer pip? Actually the issue: `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`. The lock file contains hashes. When pip parses the constraints file, does it apply hashes to the constraint? The error says flask==3.1.3 requires hashes. In pip, constraints with hashes... Let me check `pip/_internal/req/req_file.py` line 239 — the constraint file hash options.\n\nActually the issue arises because `pip install -c` with a constraint file containing hashes: pip's `Constraint` objects carry `hashes`. Then `factory.py:274` `hashes &= ireq.hashes(...)`. Hmm.\n\nWait — the error message says \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had.\" and lists flask==3.1.3 with hash f4bcbe... — that IS in the lock. Hmm, but the point is the *requirement* flask==3.1.3 came from requirements.txt which has no hash.\n\nSo `--require-hashes` mode got enabled. Why? Because the constraint file's hashes? Let's check pip's behavior: in newer pip versions, a constraint file with hashes propagates to requirements... Let me look at the pip source for how constraints with hashes are handled.\n\nActually the important thing: the Docker build uses `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`. The baseline lock file has hashes too and the base image presumably built fine. Let me check the pip version in python:3.13-slim — that's probably pip 25.x. My local pip is 25.0.1 which errors. But the venv pip 26.3 might behave differently.\n\nLet me test with the venv pip.\n\nActually wait — the failure is local pip 25.0.1 behavior. But the Docker image uses whatever pip ships with python:3.13-slim. Since the baseline was presumably working in the Docker build, it should be fine there. But I want to verify. Let me check with pip 26.3 locally first.\n\nActually more important: my new lock file was generated by pip-compile which produced hashes for `click==8.5.1` while the baseline had `click==8.5.0`. That's fine — a newer click. But hold on: pip-compile resolves against the local index which is a proxy. The hashes should match PyPI's real hashes since the proxy serves real PyPI files. Let me verify by checking that the hash for markdown-it-py 4.3.0 matches the real PyPI one.\n\nLet me test the install with the newer pip.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373905298,"end":1794373905323}}}
