{"type":"text","timestamp":1794373916770,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c905184b001hQ4ycxCcFfisny","messageID":"msg_1c904f66a001NQ7wJJ65ClYKHH","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The newer pip (26.3) works with the constraint file. The docker image uses whatever pip comes with python:3.13-slim, which is likely pip 25.x. Let me check: the base image python:3.13-slim ships pip ~25.x. The constraint file installed as `-c` triggers require_hashes mode because the constraint entries have hashes? Actually the error was about `flask==3.1.3` missing hashes — that comes from the requirements.txt file combined with the lock's hashes.\n\nWait, actually the original lock file had the same structure and the error was the same with the baseline. So the baseline itself would fail with old pip 25.0.1. Let me check whether pip in the container is newer.\n\nActually the error message said \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbe...\" meaning it wanted the hash added to the requirements file. This happens because with `-c constraints`, pip reads constraint hashes and... hmm. Actually with pip >= 25.x, using `-c` with a file containing hashes enables require_hashes mode? Let's check: in req_file.py line 239, `if opts.require_hashes: options.require_hashes = opts.require_hashes`. That only sets it if already True.\n\nHmm, but the error occurred. Let me look at how constraint files with hashes interplay. In pip, `install_req_from_parsed_requirement` for constraints... Actually the issue may be that pip's `-c` constraint file entries get their hashes attached to the requirement via `ireq.hashes()` — factory.py line 274 `hashes &= ireq.hashes(trust_internet=False)`.\n\nActually let me look at line 286 context again: `if any(req.has_hash_options for req in requirements)` — requirements here comes from parsing `-r` files AND... let me check if constraints are included.\n\nAnyway — this failed with the *baseline* lock file too (I tested with git stash and it failed the same way with system pip 25.0.1). So the base project as given already has this issue with pip 25.0.1, but pip 26.3 handles it fine. The Docker image python:3.13-slim — what pip does it ship? Let's check the digest's pip version. Python 3.13 images ship pip 25.x currently. Hmm, that's risky.\n\nActually wait — the error with pip 25.0.1 says flask==3.1.3 needs hashes. But `flask==3.1.3` is in requirements.txt WITHOUT hashes, and the lock file provides hashes. In hash-checking mode, pip requires that the requirement itself (from -r) has hashes. The constraint file `-c` entries... hmm, in pip, when a constraint has hashes and the requirement doesn't, pip combines? Let me check `install_req_from_parsed_requirement` / how hashes propagate.\n\nLet me look at pip 25.0.1's req_file.py handling of constraint files.\n\nActually, the key question: does the Docker build succeed? The Dockerfile does:\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\nWith pip 25.0.1 (in python:3.13-slim at that digest, could be 25.0.1 or newer).\n\nSince the baseline repo has this exact structure and README says the lock is \"installed as a pip constraints file\", presumably the Docker build works in the real environment. The sandbox pip is 25.0.1 which is older than what the image ships possibly.\n\nLet me check: python:3.13-slim image — pip version. In the dockerfile the image is pinned by digest. I can't pull it. Let me check the pip version in python:3.13 images around late 2025... Python 3.13.5 was released Aug 2025 with pip 25.2. The digest is for python 3.13-slim; pip in that image is likely 25.2 or 25.3.\n\nThe key difference between pip 25.0.1 and 26.3 for constraint-file hashes: pip 26.x may have relaxed/changed. Let me test with pip 25.2 to see if the baseline works.\n\nActually, a cleaner solution that avoids all this: make the Dockerfile install with `--require-hashes` from the lock file directly (lock contains everything with hashes), i.e. `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock`. But then the README contract says lock is installed as constraints file. I can modify the Dockerfile though — it's my repo. But minimizing risk: if the grader builds the image with the given Dockerfile and the base pip has this issue, the build fails entirely.\n\nLet me test pip 25.2/25.3 behavior to determine whether the baseline works there.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794373916747,"end":1794373916765}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794373937124,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a1c905126f76e1aa61f227","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && for v in 25.0.1 25.2 25.3 26.0; do python3 -m venv /workspace/.tmpv_$v >/dev/null 2>&1; /workspace/.tmpv_$v/bin/pip install -q \"pip==$v\" 2>&1 | tail -1; echo \"--- pip $v ---\"; /workspace/.tmpv_$v/bin/pip install -q --no-cache-dir --dry-run -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock 2>&1 | tail -3; done"},"output":"--- pip 25.0.1 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n--- pip 25.2 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n--- pip 25.3 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n--- pip 26.0 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n","metadata":{"output":"--- pip 25.0.1 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n--- pip 25.2 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n--- pip 25.3 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n--- pip 26.0 ---\nERROR: Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements. Here is a list of those requirements along with the hashes their downloaded archives actually had. Add lines like these to your requirements files to prevent tampering. (If you did not enable --require-hashes manually, note that it turns on automatically when any package has a hash.)\n    flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && for v in 25.0.1 25.2 25.3 26.0; do python3 -m venv /workspace/.tmpv_$v >/dev/null 2>&1; /workspace/.tmpv_$v/bin/pip install -q \"pip==$v\" 2>&1 | tail -1; echo \"--- pip $v ---\"; /workspace/.tmpv_$v/bin/pip install -q --no-cache-dir --dry-run -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock 2>&1 | tail -3; done","time":{"start":1794373916757,"end":1794373937099}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c905184e001YBeQ5zJhpvvrLp","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c904f66a001NQ7wJJ65ClYKHH"}}
