{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794374007880,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9067c36001cZE5CryKemjn0M","messageID":"msg_1c90666fc001L3ezk26xXTqQRz","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374007881,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9067c380013WOhJB3bRRqUB4","messageID":"msg_1c90666fc001L3ezk26xXTqQRz","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The lock file format issue: pip 25.x fails because when `-c` constraints file contains hashes, the requirement itself gets `--require-hashes` mode enabled? Actually the error says \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=...\" meaning flask requirement itself has hash options and pip requires hashes for all requirements. Hmm, the issue is that with pip <26.1, hash-checking mode gets enabled because the constraint file entries have hashes... Actually no — the requirements.txt is `-r` and constraints are `-c`. In pip < 26.1, constraints with hashes cause issues.\n\nWait — but the Docker image python:3.13-slim has a specific pip version. The baseline `requirements.lock` presumably worked with the Docker build. Let me check git show of the original lock file to compare format.\n\nActually the key insight: the original lock file probably had a different format. Let me check the original.\n\nActually the error occurs with the ORIGINAL lock too (I stashed and got same error with pip 25.0.1). So the baseline also fails with old pip — meaning the docker image's pip is new enough (26.1+?) OR the original lock format differs.\n\nLet me check the original lock file format fromgit.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794374007864,"end":1794374007877}}}
