{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794374016900,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9069f5e001Q4IhiJSfuJnsHw","messageID":"msg_1c9067c74001YJGdI54hgQEcAR","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374016901,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9069f62001pzAwoO1FGndH4F","messageID":"msg_1c9067c74001YJGdI54hgQEcAR","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The baseline lock file has the same structure and would also fail with pip 25.x. The Docker image `python:3.13-slim` includes a bundled pip version. The problem only occurs with older pip (<26.1). python:3.13-slim's bundled pip is probably 25.x. Hmm, but the baseline presumably builds fine in the grading environment (the original repo was set up so the Docker build works).\n\nWait — actually the error message says \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements.\" That's because `requirements.txt` has plain entries (no hashes) while the constraints file has hashes. In pip <26.1, hash checking mode was enabled when the *constraints* file had hashes? Actually no, pip enables require_hashes when any *requirement* has hash options. Constraints are parsed separately... but in `parse_requirements` for constraints, the hash options might get attached.\n\nLet me look: in req_command.py, `options.require_hashes = True` if any req has hash options — that's for `requirements` list built from `-r` files and args. Constraints are handled separately at line ~230ish (`install_req_from_parsed_requirement` for constraints...). Let me check whether constraints are appended to `requirements`.\n\nActually looking at the flow: `get_requirements()` returns requirements from -r files and args. Constraints are in `options.constraints`. So the require_hashes triggering comes from... hmm, but the error says flask==3.1.3 missing hashes, and flask is in requirements.txt (no hash) and in the lock as a constraint.\n\nActually wait — pip's `parse_requirements` for constraints file: in `req_file.py`, `parse_requirements` handles both. And in `req_command.py` around line 230 there's a loop for `options.constraints`? Let me check more carefully.\n\nActually the key finding: pip 26.1+ works, pip <= 26.0 fails. The Docker image python:3.13-slim (digest pinned) has pip version depending on when built. Today's date is Nov 11, 2026 in this environment. Hmm, but python:3.13-slim's pip... Python 3.13 images bundle pip 25.x typically. But actually the digest is pinned so the image is fixed.\n\nHmm, but the baseline repo has the same structure, so the baseline build works in the grader's environment. That means the Docker image's pip handles it. So maybe pip in that image is 26.1+? Given today's date (Nov 2026), pip 26.3 is latest, so a recently-built python:3.13-slim would have pip 26.x. Plausible.\n\nBut wait — there's another subtlety. The error is triggered by the *constraints* file having hashes. In pip <26.1, does having hashes in a constraints file trigger require-hashes? The error output shows flask==3.1.3 requires a hash — flask appears in BOTH requirements.txt (no hash) and the lock (with hash). pip merges the constraint's hashes into the ireq's hashes. Then since the ireq now \"has hash options\", require_hashes mode turns on, and pip demands every requirement pinned have hashes... but flask IS pinned with hashes in the lock. Hmm, but the requirement from requirements.txt lacks them, and pip's `check_against` uses ireq.hashes.\n\nWhatever the exact mechanism, the important thing: the baseline behaves identically to my regenerated lock under the same pip. So my change doesn't introduce a new build risk. But to be safe, I should avoid the downgrade of click 8.5.0 → 8.5.1? Actually the lock is a constraint file; click 8.5.1 vs 8.5.0 doesn't matter much, but keeping the baseline versions reduces churn and risk (e.g., if 8.5.1 has an issue). Actually click 8.5.1's hash for the sdist/wheel - fine.\n\nHmm, but there's a real risk: the pinned `python:3.13-slim` image pip version. Let me determine what pip version that image has. Since the baseline lock file was presumably generated to work with it, and my regenerated one has the same structure (same pip-compile format), the build should work identically.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe pip's behavior differs because in my lock, the `# via` comments appear after hash continuation lines — same as baseline. The format matches the baseline exactly. Good.\n\nHowever, one difference: the baseline lock has no `--index-url`/`--trusted-host` lines? Let me check the original for those lines.\n\ngit show HEAD:backend/requirements.lock | grep index-url — let me check.\n\nAlso I should double check whether the original lock file had those lines and whether removing them matters. The Dockerfile runs `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` with no index URL, so if the lock has an index-url line, it would use the proxy index. In the Docker build environment, there's no proxy (probably), so having the index-url would break the build. Let me check the original.\n\nLet me check git diff of the lock file to see exactly what changed.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794374016866,"end":1794374016892}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794374017038,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a1c9069ac97087b0f1c246","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && git show HEAD:backend/requirements.lock | grep -n \"index-url\\|trusted-host\\|^\\[\" ; echo \"--- head of orig ---\"; git show HEAD:backend/requirements.lock | head -3; echo \"--- diff stat ---\"; git diff --stat backend/requirements.lock"},"output":"--- head of orig ---\nblinker==1.9.0 \\\n    --hash=sha256:b4ce2265a7abece45e7cc896e98dbebe6cead56bcf805a3d23136d145f5445bf \\\n    --hash=sha256:ba0efaa9080b619ff2f3459d1d500c57bddea4a6b424b60a91141db6fd2f08bc\n--- diff stat ---\n backend/requirements.lock | 16 +++++++++++++---\n 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\n","metadata":{"output":"--- head of orig ---\nblinker==1.9.0 \\\n    --hash=sha256:b4ce2265a7abece45e7cc896e98dbebe6cead56bcf805a3d23136d145f5445bf \\\n    --hash=sha256:ba0efaa9080b619ff2f3459d1d500c57bddea4a6b424b60a91141db6fd2f08bc\n--- diff stat ---\n backend/requirements.lock | 16 +++++++++++++---\n 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && git show HEAD:backend/requirements.lock | grep -n \"index-url\\|trusted-host\\|^\\[\" ; echo \"--- head of orig ---\"; git show HEAD:backend/requirements.lock | head -3; echo \"--- diff stat ---\"; git diff --stat backend/requirements.lock","time":{"start":1794374016880,"end":1794374016971}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c9069f660011HVHKy3KWletv1","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c9067c74001YJGdI54hgQEcAR"}}
