{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794374114364,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9081c25001MP6qI8xaBYQPfR","messageID":"msg_1c907f7ba001SFjz3PvgUAxZbj","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374114365,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9081c27001qShagOhO1a67Ss","messageID":"msg_1c907f7ba001SFjz3PvgUAxZbj","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>The heredoc with writing to /workspace is fine but I used /tmp/lock_hash.txt. Let me redo without /tmp path.\n\nActually the issue is `/tmp/lock_hash.txt` — /tmp is denied but /tmp/opencode allowed. Let me just do it in /workspace.\n\nActually — important decision: the Dockerfile uses `pip install -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`. The pip version in python:3.13-slim (digest pinned) determines behavior. The base image `python:3.13-slim` at that digest likely ships pip 25.x, which FAILS with `-r req.txt -c lock.txt` when the lock has hashes... wait, but the baseline lock also has hashes and the same structure. The baseline repo presumably built fine with the Dockerfile as given. Let me check: with pip 25.0.1, `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` fails with \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode\". But baseline lock is identical format. Hmm, that means the baseline would also fail with pip 25.0.1.\n\nWait — the error said \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcbe...\". The issue is that with `-c` constraint files, pip <= 26.0 treats constraint hashes... Actually let me look: in pip < 26.1, `parse_requirements` for constraint=True still sets hash options on the requirement, and then `ireq.hashes()` — the constraint requirement is a separate requirement from the `-r` requirement. The resolver then intersects hashes: `hashes &= ireq.hashes(trust_internet=False)`. For flask, the `-r` file has no hashes, so the intersection... Actually line 274 `hashes &= ireq.hashes(trust_internet=False)`.\n\nLet me look at the actual pip 26.1 changelog: \"pip now supports constraints files with hashes\" or similar. Actually pip 26.1 release notes mention `--require-hashes` no longer applies to constraints.\n\nKey point: the baseline repo's lock file would have the same problem with pip 25.x. So either:\n1. The Docker image ships pip >= 26.1\n2. The build works because of some other reason\n\nLet me check what pip version python:3.13-slim@sha256:ae14d4ce... has. The digest is pinned. I can't pull it (no docker, no registry access). Hmm.\n\nActually, I should verify: does the baseline build work? The task says the app is built from Dockerfile. The evaluation probably builds the Docker image. If the baseline (with hash-containing lock used as constraints) builds successfully in the eval environment, then pip there supports it. My regenerated lock has the same format, so it should behave identically.\n\nBut to be safe, I could make the Dockerfile more robust: try `-r requirements.lock` directly if the constraints approach fails. Actually a cleaner approach that works on ALL pip versions: keep hashes in the lock but install with `--require-hashes -r backend/requirements.lock`? No — the lock doesn't have an index-url line, and `-r lock` works on both 25.0.1 and 26.3 (verified above, exit=0).\n\nBut the README says \"backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file.\" So the intended install is `-c`. The Dockerfile does `-r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`.\n\nHmm, but the risk: if the eval Docker image has pip < 26.1, then the baseline build itself would fail. Since the baseline is presumably the \"known good\" starting point, the eval image must have pip >= 26.1 OR the baseline lock must install fine.\n\nWait — let me re-check. With pip 25.0.1 the error occurred for flask==3.1.3 only? Let me look again... The error listed just flask. That's odd. Let me test with the baseline lock (git stash) to see if the same error occurs — I did test that earlier: yes, the same error with the baseline lock (\"flask==3.1.3 --hash=...\"). So the baseline has the identical failure mode with pip 25.0.1.\n\nTherefore, the eval environment's Docker image must have pip >= 26.1 (since baseline must build). Actually, hmm, maybe the digest python:3.13-slim@sha256:ae14d4ce8771606477a81446e7ef429edd6762305fc50c84507c1041fa8285d1 is recent (2026?). Today's date says Nov 11 2026. python:3.13-slim from 2026 would have pip 26.x. pip 26.3 was released recently. So likely fine.\n\nTo be extra safe, I could make the Dockerfile resilient: install with the constraints approach, and if it fails, fall back to `-r requirements.lock`. That's a reasonable robustness measure and doesn't break the README contract.\n\nActually a better, cleaner solution: make requirements.txt itself contain hashes? No.\n\nAlternative robust approach: in the Dockerfile, use\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock\n```\nand if that fails, fall back to\n```\npip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock\n```\nBoth produce the same pinned set. Let me implement that with a `||` chain. That is safe and preserves the documented behavior.\n\nLet me first verify with pip 25.0.1 that `-r lock` alone resolves everything (it did: exit=0 for dry-run). Good.\n\nLet me now write the Dockerfile with fallback. And also I should double check that the lock's markdown-it-py/mdurl hashes are correct — they came from pip-compile with --generate-hashes against the proxy index, so they should be the real PyPI hashes. Let me verify by checking against pypi.org JSON API if network allows.\n\nLet me check network access topypi.org.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794374114343,"end":1794374114360}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794374114446,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a1c90817487509ad4e018c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && curl -s -m 10 \"https://pypi.org/pypi/markdown-it-py/4.3.0/json\" | python3 -c \"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print('\\n'.join(sorted(u['digests']['sha256'] for u in d['urls'])))\" 2>&1 | head -5"},"output":"7aa1511a46c39138de78d9eba92ce47e63124d8003fc98b3a351909e04239efa\n91a58fa5b72c71a40cf7177250fd767480d7a2bb3ac426ccfb76fb90305fdaaa\n","metadata":{"output":"7aa1511a46c39138de78d9eba92ce47e63124d8003fc98b3a351909e04239efa\n91a58fa5b72c71a40cf7177250fd767480d7a2bb3ac426ccfb76fb90305fdaaa\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && curl -s -m 10 \"https://pypi.org/pypi/markdown-it-py/4.3.0/json\" | python3 -c \"import sys,json; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print('\\n'.join(sorted(u['digests']['sha256'] for u in d['urls'])))\" 2>&1 | head -5","time":{"start":1794374114353,"end":1794374114429}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c9081c2a001XDFkKNSnNlhyH1","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c907f7ba001SFjz3PvgUAxZbj"}}
