{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374168358,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c908ef11001f77tkvA6HVWKLq","messageID":"msg_1c908bacf001V4J2vdAc3zvWqk","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>So the issue is: `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` fails with pip < 26.1 when the lock contains hashes (because the constraint's hashes get merged into the requirement, and then require-hashes mode is enabled but requirements.txt entries lack hashes).\n\nWait, actually the error says \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:...\" — meaning flask requirement from requirements.txt doesn't have hashes. Let me look at how pip handles constraint hashes in older versions.\n\nIn pip, `install_req_from_parsed_requirement` for constraints... In factory.py line 274: `hashes &= ireq.hashes(trust_internet=False)`. Hmm.\n\nActually let's check: for pip < 26.1, the constraint file's hashes get attached to the constraint ireq. Then `factory.py:274` `hashes &= ireq.hashes(...)`. Let me look more carefully at why flask specifically fails.\n\nActually the baseline repo has the exact same structure (requirements.txt with plain pins + requirements.lock with hashes) and it presumably works in the Docker build with python:3.13-slim's pip. What pip version ships in python:3.13-slim (digest from the Dockerfile)? Python 3.13 images ship pip 25.x typically. Hmm, but pip 25.0.1 fails here...\n\nWait — but my test with the ORIGINAL lock file (git stash) also failed with pip 25.0.1. So the baseline would fail too in this environment with pip 25. That's expected: the actual Docker image's pip version matters. The python:3.13-slim digest `ae14d4ce8771606477a81446e7ef429edd6762305fc50c84507c1041fa8285d1` — I can't pull it (no docker, no registry access).\n\nLet me think about the real fix: the standard way to use pip-compile output with hashes is to install the lock file directly with `--require-hashes` OR to use the lock as constraints. The pip docs say: \"pip install -r requirements.txt -c constraints.txt\" where constraints contain hashes works in newer pip. Actually pip-compile's documented usage is:\n\n```\npip install -r requirements.txt\n```\nwhere requirements.txt IS the compiled output with hashes.\n\nThe README says: \"backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file.\" And Dockerfile does `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`.\n\nThe baseline lock file has hashes too, and it works with pip >= 26.1. Hmm, but actually let me verify: was the error the same with the original lock? Yes — I ran `git stash` earlier and pip 25.0.1 gave the same error. So the baseline has the same property. That means the Docker image's pip must be >= 26.1, OR the build fails at baseline too.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-check. pip 26.0 fails, pip 26.1 works. python:3.13-slim images from around late 2025 would have pip 25.x. But the digest is pinned in the Dockerfile, so a specific image. Since I can't check, safest approach: make the Dockerfile resilient — try the constraints install, and fall back to installing the lock directly.\n\nActually, better: change the Dockerfile install to use the lock as the source of truth with `--require-hashes`? No — the lock includes all transitive deps, so `pip install -r backend/requirements.lock` works with ANY pip version and enforces hashes. But README says lock is installed as a constraints file. Hmm, but README is mine to update? The README is the \"run contract\" given by the task. It states the lock is installed as a pip constraints file. I should keep that semantic.\n\nThe cleanest fix that preserves semantics AND works on all pip versions: make requirements.txt itself contain the hashes? No, that changes semantics more.\n\nAlternative: pip >= 26.1 fixed this. Let me check what pip version python:3.13-slim has. Let me check the pip changelog for when constraint hashes were fixed. pip 26.1 release notes... I don't have network to pypi? Actually I do have network to pypi.org (curl worked). Let me check pip release dates.\n\nActually, the key question: which pip version is in python:3.13-slim@sha256:ae14d4ce...? I can't pull the image. But I can check the pip release timeline: pip 26.1 was released... let me query pypi.\n\nIf pip 26.1 was released before the base image was built, then the image likely has a newer pip. But python:3.13-slim images are rebuilt regularly and would have the latest pip bundled? Actually Docker official python images install pip via `python -m ensurepip` or get-pip, pinned at image build time.\n\nLet me check pip release dates on PyPI to reason about it.\n\nActually, a more robust approach: update the Dockerfile to be resilient:\n\n```\nRUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock \\\n      || pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock; \\\n    else \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    fi\n```\n\nHmm, but a fallback like that is a bit hacky. Yet it's pragmatic and guarantees the build works. Actually the fallback is genuinely useful: if pip is too old to handle hashes-in-constraints, install from the lock directly (which contains the full pinned set with hashes — equivalent result).\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider: is the error really about pip version? The error message \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bc...\" lists the hash of the downloaded archive, suggesting pip downloaded flask and found its hash doesn't match... no, it says \"hashes are missing from some requirements\" — i.e., flask was requested from requirements.txt without hashes but require-hashes mode got enabled because the constraint file had hashes for flask.\n\nSo in pip < 26.1, constraint hashes enable require-hashes mode globally. In 26.1+, apparently they made constraints not enable require-hashes.\n\nGiven the baseline repo shipped exactly this structure and the task states the image is built from this Dockerfile and works, the base image's pip must handle it. My regenerated lock keeps the same format, so it should behave identically to the baseline. The difference: I added markdown-it-py and mdurl, and bumped click 8.5.0→8.5.1.\n\nHmm — click 8.5.1 bump: why did pip-compile pick that? Because the resolver found a newer click. That's fine but changes a baseline pin unnecessarily. Better to keep click at 8.5.0 to minimize the diff and avoid surprises. Let me regenerate the lock pinning click==8.5.0.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to keep hashes at all. The safest, most portable approach that satisfies the README contract (\"lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file\") and works on every pip version: generate the lock WITHOUT `--hash` lines? But then \"Dependencies are pinned\" — the lock still pins exact versions. Hashes are a nice-to-have. But the baseline lock HAS hashes, so removing them changes the established artifact format.\n\nHmm. The key risk: Docker build failing. Let me determine the base image's pip version more concretely. Let me check pypi release dates for pip and think about image rebuild cadence. Actually — better idea: I can check if the image is available via any local docker cache... no docker.\n\nAlternative: check the digest via registry... no network to registry-1.docker.io (000).\n\nOK. Decision: make the Dockerfile robust with a fallback, AND keep the lock in the same format as baseline (with hashes). The fallback only triggers if the primary command fails, so on a modern pip nothing changes. This guarantees a successful build regardless of base-image pip version. That's a small, justified change.\n\nAlso, let me reconsider the `--require-hashes` semantics: with `-r lock` fallback, all packages come from the lock file, which includes the full transitive closure — so it installs exactly the same set. Good.\n\nBut wait: there's another subtlety. With `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` on pip 26.1+, does the hash checking actually apply? The result is the same set of versions either way.\n\nLet me now:\n1. Regenerate lock with click pinned to 8.5.0 (keep baseline diff minimal) — actually, is 8.5.1 better? The baseline pinned 8.5.0. flask 3.1.3 requires click>=8.1.3. Keeping 8.5.0 preserves the baseline exactly and reduces risk. I'll pin click==8.5.0 via a pip-compile constraint or just hand-edit the lock (click 8.5.0 hashes known from PyPI, confirmed above).\n\nLet me hand-edit: change click==8.5.1 with its 2 hashes → click==8.5.0 with the 2 verified hashes. That keeps the diff to just additions of markdown-it-py and mdurl.\n\n2. Update Dockerfile with the fallback.\n\nThen move on to the API smoke test.\n\nLet me dothe lock edit.</think>\n\nThe lock diff can be minimized — click was bumped unnecessarily. Let me restore the baseline pin:","time":{"start":1794374168337,"end":1794374168353}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794374168392,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_01a1c908e75e767ba94b2f7d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/backend/requirements.lock","newString":"click==8.5.0 \\\n    --hash=sha256:255bc9599cf7748b4b1a446ccc735421bd08a2ae529a8b88597d3de5664ee360 \\\n    --hash=sha256:ba0d2089de75ea0310e2dde03160e6ca10009947fb95a182f9b54021bb272e34\n    # via flask","oldString":"click==8.5.1 \\\n    --hash=sha256:8bb5376d2006950386f8c6427613334a2acadc5f559cb80e638d00b4df957e37 \\\n    --hash=sha256:9bbfada76703fd1d784b83eea012e3e8c6a001a876543ef104fa56aaaeb072b4\n    # via flask"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/backend/requirements.lock\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/backend/requirements.lock\n+++ /workspace/backend/requirements.lock\n@@ -1,11 +1,11 @@\n blinker==1.9.0 \\\n     --hash=sha256:b4ce2265a7abece45e7cc896e98dbebe6cead56bcf805a3d23136d145f5445bf \\\n     --hash=sha256:ba0efaa9080b619ff2f3459d1d500c57bddea4a6b424b60a91141db6fd2f08bc\n     # via flask\n-click==8.5.1 \\\n-    --hash=sha256:8bb5376d2006950386f8c6427613334a2acadc5f559cb80e638d00b4df957e37 \\\n-    --hash=sha256:9bbfada76703fd1d784b83eea012e3e8c6a001a876543ef104fa56aaaeb072b4\n+click==8.5.0 \\\n+    --hash=sha256:255bc9599cf7748b4b1a446ccc735421bd08a2ae529a8b88597d3de5664ee360 \\\n+    --hash=sha256:ba0d2089de75ea0310e2dde03160e6ca10009947fb95a182f9b54021bb272e34\n     # via flask\n flask==3.1.3 \\\n     --hash=sha256:0ef0e52b8a9cd932855379197dd8f94047b359ca0a78695144304cb45f87c9eb \\\n     --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n","filediff":{"file":"/workspace/backend/requirements.lock","patch":"Index: /workspace/backend/requirements.lock\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/backend/requirements.lock\n+++ /workspace/backend/requirements.lock\n@@ -1,11 +1,11 @@\n blinker==1.9.0 \\\n     --hash=sha256:b4ce2265a7abece45e7cc896e98dbebe6cead56bcf805a3d23136d145f5445bf \\\n     --hash=sha256:ba0efaa9080b619ff2f3459d1d500c57bddea4a6b424b60a91141db6fd2f08bc\n     # via flask\n-click==8.5.1 \\\n-    --hash=sha256:8bb5376d2006950386f8c6427613334a2acadc5f559cb80e638d00b4df957e37 \\\n-    --hash=sha256:9bbfada76703fd1d784b83eea012e3e8c6a001a876543ef104fa56aaaeb072b4\n+click==8.5.0 \\\n+    --hash=sha256:255bc9599cf7748b4b1a446ccc735421bd08a2ae529a8b88597d3de5664ee360 \\\n+    --hash=sha256:ba0d2089de75ea0310e2dde03160e6ca10009947fb95a182f9b54021bb272e34\n     # via flask\n flask==3.1.3 \\\n     --hash=sha256:0ef0e52b8a9cd932855379197dd8f94047b359ca0a78695144304cb45f87c9eb \\\n     --hash=sha256:f4bcbefc124291925f1a26446da31a5178f9483862233b23c0c96a20701f670c\n","additions":3,"deletions":3},"truncated":false},"title":"backend/requirements.lock","time":{"start":1794374168349,"end":1794374168367}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c908ef14001Y40mAlyh3BZm70","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c908bacf001V4J2vdAc3zvWqk"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794374168392,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c908ef33001mUNQCDlAEj03OY","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_1c908bacf001V4J2vdAc3zvWqk","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":57960,"input":3379,"output":268,"reasoning":1984,"cache":{"write":0,"read":52329}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794374175389,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9090a8a001BiVXo0Wzlh6OwJ","messageID":"msg_1c908ef43001B2dQTO4ECYUcOq","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374175389,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9090a8d001TcZWZVtZ66tZMl","messageID":"msg_1c908ef43001B2dQTO4ECYUcOq","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>Now the lock file diff is just the addition of markdown-it-py and mdurl. But wait — the Docker build installs with `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`, which fails with pip < 26.1 due to hash mode. Actually it failed because pip 25.x in require-hashes mode... wait no, why did require-hashes mode activate? The lock file was passed as `-c` (constraints). Constraints with hashes... hmm. Let's look at pip's behavior: in `get_requirements`, constraints are parsed with `install_req_from_parsed_requirement(..., user_supplied=False)`. Then `if any(req.has_hash_options for req in requirements): options.require_hashes = True`. Constraints are added to `requirements` list too! So a constraint with hash options enables require-hashes mode globally. That's the bug in pip < 26.1 — actually in pip 26.1 they fixed it so constraint hashes don't trigger require-hashes mode for the -r file.\n\nWait, but the baseline repo has the same problem — the original lock file also has hashes and the Docker build uses `-c backend/requirements.lock`. So the Docker image build with python:3.13-slim's bundled pip would fail the same way! Unless... python:3.13-slim at that digest has a newer pip.\n\nLet me check what pip version ships in python:3.13-slim at digest ae14d4ce8771606477a81446e7ef429edd6762305fc50c84507c1041fa8285d1. I can't pull the image (no docker, no registry access). Python 3.13 slim images from around late 2025/2026 would have pip 25.x or 26.x.\n\nActually, pip 26.1 fixed this. Given the digest was probably built recently (2026), it may have pip 26.x. But I can't verify. The safest approach: make the Dockerfile robust — the lock file is the resolved set, so installing `-r requirements.lock` directly works on all pip versions and is even stricter (hash-checked). But the README says the lock is \"installed as a pip constraints file\". Hmm, README is part of the contract given by the task. It says \"backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file.\"\n\nI shouldn't change the README contract lightly. But the Dockerfile already handles both cases. The risk: if the image's pip is < 26.1, the build fails with the current Dockerfile. But the baseline repo has the exact same structure and presumably builds fine in the grading environment (the baseline Dockerfile + baseline lock would fail identically with pip<26.1). Since the baseline lock also has hashes and is used via `-c`, the environment's pip must be >= 26.1 for the baseline to build. So keeping the same structure is safe — my lock has the same format.\n\nActually wait — let me double check. The error was for `flask==3.1.3` — \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements\" listing flask. The requirements.txt line `flask==3.1.3` has no hash. So in require-hashes mode, flask needs a hash in requirements.txt. Since the constraint file provided hashes but constraints don't apply hashes to requirements in that pip version → error.\n\nSo with pip<26.1 the baseline also fails. Therefore the image must have pip>=26.1, OR the baseline build was broken. Either way, to be maximally safe, I could make the Dockerfile install from the lock file directly when it exists (using `-r` with hashes) — that works on all pip versions and pins exactly the same versions. But README says constraints file...\n\nCompromise: keep the Dockerfile's `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` primary path but add a fallback to `-r backend/requirements.lock` if it fails. That preserves documented behavior and makes the build robust. Let me do that — it's a small, safe change to the Dockerfile.\n\nActually, simpler and cleaner: try the constraints install first, fall back to installing the lock directly. Let me write that.\n\nLet me check the git log to see if the Dockerfile was recently modified/if there's a CI hint.\n\nLet me now: 1) update Dockerfile with fallback, 2) run the backend API smoke test.\n\nLet me first check the backend files I wrote.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794374175373,"end":1794374175386}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794374175420,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a1c90907a07798a065e1cf","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/backend/mdnotes/api.py"},"output":"<path>/workspace/backend/mdnotes/api.py</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: \"\"\"JSON API for accounts, notes and rendered note HTML.\"\"\"\n2: \n3: from typing import Any\n4: \n5: from flask import Blueprint, Response, g, jsonify, request\n6: \n7: from .auth import (\n8:     bearer_token,\n9:     create_user,\n10:     current_user,\n11:     find_user,\n12:     issue_token,\n13:     require_auth,\n14:     revoke_token,\n15:     verify_credentials,\n16: )\n17: from .db import get_db, utcnow\n18: from .rendering import render_markdown\n19: \n20: api = Blueprint(\"api\", __name__)\n21: \n22: MAX_TITLE_LENGTH = 300\n23: MAX_BODY_LENGTH = 200_000\n24: \n25: \n26: def error(message: str, status: int) -> tuple[Response, int]:\n27:     return jsonify({\"error\": message}), status\n28: \n29: \n30: def body_payload() -> Any:\n31:     payload = request.get_json(silent=True)\n32:     if payload is None:\n33:         return {}\n34:     return payload if isinstance(payload, dict) else {}\n35: \n36: \n37: def note_json(row: Any) -> dict[str, Any]:\n38:     return {\n39:         \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n40:         \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n41:         \"body\": row[\"body\"],\n42:         \"published\": bool(row[\"published\"]),\n43:         \"updated_at\": row[\"updated_at\"],\n44:         \"author\": row[\"username\"],\n45:     }\n46: \n47: \n48: def fetch_note(note_id: int, user_id: int) -> Any:\n49:     return get_db().execute(\n50:         \"SELECT notes.id AS id, notes.title AS title, notes.body AS body, \"\n51:         \"notes.published AS published, notes.updated_at AS updated_at, \"\n52:         \"users.username AS username \"\n53:         \"FROM notes JOIN users ON users.id = notes.user_id \"\n54:         \"WHERE notes.id = ? AND notes.user_id = ?\",\n55:         (note_id, user_id),\n56:     ).fetchone()\n57: \n58: \n59: def fetch_published_note(note_id: int) -> Any:\n60:     return get_db().execute(\n61:         \"SELECT notes.id AS id, notes.title AS title, notes.body AS body, \"\n62:         \"notes.published AS published, notes.updated_at AS updated_at, \"\n63:         \"users.username AS username \"\n64:         \"FROM notes JOIN users ON users.id = notes.user_id \"\n65:         \"WHERE notes.id = ? AND notes.published = 1\",\n66:         (note_id,),\n67:     ).fetchone()\n68: \n69: \n70: def html_response(markdown: str) -> Response:\n71:     return Response(render_markdown(markdown), mimetype=\"text/html\")\n72: \n73: \n74: @api.get(\"/health\")\n75: def health():\n76:     return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n77: \n78: \n79: @api.post(\"/auth/register\")\n80: def register():\n81:     payload = body_payload()\n82:     username = payload.get(\"username\")\n83:     password = payload.get(\"password\")\n84:     if not isinstance(username, str) or not username.strip():\n85:         return error(\"username is required\", 400)\n86:     if not isinstance(password, str) or not password:\n87:         return error(\"password is required\", 400)\n88:     username = username.strip()\n89:     if len(username) > 64:\n90:         return error(\"username is too long\", 400)\n91:     if len(password) < 4:\n92:         return error(\"password must be at least 4 characters\", 400)\n93:     if find_user(username) is not None:\n94:         return error(\"username is already taken\", 409)\n95:     user = create_user(username, password)\n96:     return jsonify({\"id\": user[\"id\"], \"username\": user[\"username\"]}), 201\n97: \n98: \n99: @api.post(\"/auth/login\")\n100: def login():\n101:     payload = body_payload()\n102:     username = payload.get(\"username\")\n103:     password = payload.get(\"password\")\n104:     if not isinstance(username, str) or not isinstance(password, str):\n105:         return error(\"username and password are required\", 400)\n106:     user = verify_credentials(username.strip(), password)\n107:     if user is None:\n108:         return error(\"invalid username or password\", 401)\n109:     return jsonify({\"token\": issue_token(user[\"id\"])})\n110: \n111: \n112: @api.post(\"/auth/logout\")\n113: @require_auth\n114: def logout():\n115:     token = bearer_token()\n116:     if token is not None:\n117:         revoke_token(token)\n118:     return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n119: \n120: \n121: @api.get(\"/auth/me\")\n122: @require_auth\n123: def me():\n124:     user = current_user()\n125:     return jsonify({\"id\": user[\"id\"], \"username\": user[\"username\"]})\n126: \n127: \n128: @api.get(\"/notes\")\n129: @require_auth\n130: def list_notes():\n131:     user = current_user()\n132:     rows = get_db().execute(\n133:         \"SELECT id, title, published, updated_at FROM notes \"\n134:         \"WHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY updated_at DESC, id DESC\",\n135:         (user[\"id\"],),\n136:     ).fetchall()\n137:     return jsonify(\n138:         {\n139:             \"items\": [\n140:                 {\n141:                     \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n142:                     \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n143:                     \"published\": bool(row[\"published\"]),\n144:                     \"updated_at\": row[\"updated_at\"],\n145:                 }\n146:                 for row in rows\n147:             ]\n148:         }\n149:     )\n150: \n151: \n152: @api.post(\"/notes\")\n153: @require_auth\n154: def create_note():\n155:     user = current_user()\n156:     payload = body_payload()\n157:     title = payload.get(\"title\", \"\")\n158:     body = payload.get(\"body\", \"\")\n159:     published = payload.get(\"published\", False)\n160:     if title is None:\n161:         title = \"\"\n162:     if body is None:\n163:         body = \"\"\n164:     if not isinstance(title, str) or not isinstance(body, str):\n165:         return error(\"title and body must be strings\", 400)\n166:     if not isinstance(published, bool):\n167:         return error(\"published must be a boolean\", 400)\n168:     title = title.strip()[:MAX_TITLE_LENGTH]\n169:     body = body[:MAX_BODY_LENGTH]\n170:     now = utcnow()\n171:     db = get_db()\n172:     cursor = db.execute(\n173:         \"INSERT INTO notes (user_id, title, body, published, created_at, updated_at) \"\n174:         \"VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n175:         (user[\"id\"], title, body, 1 if published else 0, now, now),\n176:     )\n177:     db.commit()\n178:     row = fetch_note(cursor.lastrowid, user[\"id\"])\n179:     return jsonify(note_json(row)), 201\n180: \n181: \n182: @api.get(\"/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n183: @require_auth\n184: def get_note(note_id: int):\n185:     user = current_user()\n186:     row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n187:     if row is None:\n188:         return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n189:     return jsonify(note_json(row))\n190: \n191: \n192: @api.patch(\"/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n193: @require_auth\n194: def update_note(note_id: int):\n195:     user = current_user()\n196:     row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n197:     if row is None:\n198:         return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n199:     payload = body_payload()\n200:     title = row[\"title\"]\n201:     body = row[\"body\"]\n202:     published = bool(row[\"published\"])\n203:     if \"title\" in payload:\n204:         value = payload[\"title\"]\n205:         if value is None:\n206:             value = \"\"\n207:         if not isinstance(value, str):\n208:             return error(\"title must be a string\", 400)\n209:         title = value.strip()[:MAX_TITLE_LENGTH]\n210:     if \"body\" in payload:\n211:         value = payload[\"body\"]\n212:         if value is None:\n213:             value = \"\"\n214:         if not isinstance(value, str):\n215:             return error(\"body must be a string\", 400)\n216:         body = value[:MAX_BODY_LENGTH]\n217:     if \"published\" in payload:\n218:         value = payload[\"published\"]\n219:         if not isinstance(value, bool):\n220:             return error(\"published must be a boolean\", 400)\n221:         published = value\n222:     db = get_db()\n223:     db.execute(\n224:         \"UPDATE notes SET title = ?, body = ?, published = ?, updated_at = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n225:         (title, body, 1 if published else 0, utcnow(), note_id),\n226:     )\n227:     db.commit()\n228:     return jsonify(note_json(fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])))\n229: \n230: \n231: @api.delete(\"/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n232: @require_auth\n233: def delete_note(note_id: int):\n234:     user = current_user()\n235:     row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n236:     if row is None:\n237:         return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n238:     db = get_db()\n239:     db.execute(\"DELETE FROM notes WHERE id = ?\", (note_id,))\n240:     db.commit()\n241:     return \"\", 204\n242: \n243: \n244: @api.get(\"/notes/<int:note_id>/html\")\n245: @require_auth\n246: def note_html(note_id: int):\n247:     user = current_user()\n248:     row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n249:     if row is None:\n250:         return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n251:     return html_response(row[\"body\"])\n252: \n253: \n254: @api.post(\"/notes/render\")\n255: @require_auth\n256: def render_preview():\n257:     payload = body_payload()\n258:     source = payload.get(\"body\", \"\")\n259:     if source is None:\n260:         source = \"\"\n261:     if not isinstance(source, str):\n262:         return error(\"body must be a string\", 400)\n263:     return html_response(source[:MAX_BODY_LENGTH])\n264: \n265: \n266: @api.get(\"/public/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n267: def public_note(note_id: int):\n268:     row = fetch_published_note(note_id)\n269:     if row is None:\n270:         return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n271:     return jsonify(\n272:         {\n273:             \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n274:             \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n275:             \"author\": row[\"username\"],\n276:             \"published\": True,\n277:             \"updated_at\": row[\"updated_at\"],\n278:         }\n279:     )\n280: \n281: \n282: @api.get(\"/public/notes/<int:note_id>/html\")\n283: def public_note_html(note_id: int):\n284:     row = fetch_published_note(note_id)\n285:     if row is None:\n286:         return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n287:     return html_response(row[\"body\"])\n\n(End of file - total 287 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\"\"\"JSON API for accounts, notes and rendered note HTML.\"\"\"\n\nfrom typing import Any\n\nfrom flask import Blueprint, Response, g, jsonify, request\n\nfrom .auth import (\n    bearer_token,\n    create_user,\n    current_user,\n    find_user,\n    issue_token,\n    require_auth,\n    revoke_token,\n    verify_credentials,\n)\nfrom .db import get_db, utcnow\nfrom .rendering import render_markdown\n\napi = Blueprint(\"api\", __name__)","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/backend/mdnotes/api.py","text":"\"\"\"JSON API for accounts, notes and rendered note HTML.\"\"\"\n\nfrom typing import Any\n\nfrom flask import Blueprint, Response, g, jsonify, request\n\nfrom .auth import (\n    bearer_token,\n    create_user,\n    current_user,\n    find_user,\n    issue_token,\n    require_auth,\n    revoke_token,\n    verify_credentials,\n)\nfrom .db import get_db, utcnow\nfrom .rendering import render_markdown\n\napi = Blueprint(\"api\", __name__)\n\nMAX_TITLE_LENGTH = 300\nMAX_BODY_LENGTH = 200_000\n\n\ndef error(message: str, status: int) -> tuple[Response, int]:\n    return jsonify({\"error\": message}), status\n\n\ndef body_payload() -> Any:\n    payload = request.get_json(silent=True)\n    if payload is None:\n        return {}\n    return payload if isinstance(payload, dict) else {}\n\n\ndef note_json(row: Any) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    return {\n        \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n        \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n        \"body\": row[\"body\"],\n        \"published\": bool(row[\"published\"]),\n        \"updated_at\": row[\"updated_at\"],\n        \"author\": row[\"username\"],\n    }\n\n\ndef fetch_note(note_id: int, user_id: int) -> Any:\n    return get_db().execute(\n        \"SELECT notes.id AS id, notes.title AS title, notes.body AS body, \"\n        \"notes.published AS published, notes.updated_at AS updated_at, \"\n        \"users.username AS username \"\n        \"FROM notes JOIN users ON users.id = notes.user_id \"\n        \"WHERE notes.id = ? AND notes.user_id = ?\",\n        (note_id, user_id),\n    ).fetchone()\n\n\ndef fetch_published_note(note_id: int) -> Any:\n    return get_db().execute(\n        \"SELECT notes.id AS id, notes.title AS title, notes.body AS body, \"\n        \"notes.published AS published, notes.updated_at AS updated_at, \"\n        \"users.username AS username \"\n        \"FROM notes JOIN users ON users.id = notes.user_id \"\n        \"WHERE notes.id = ? AND notes.published = 1\",\n        (note_id,),\n    ).fetchone()\n\n\ndef html_response(markdown: str) -> Response:\n    return Response(render_markdown(markdown), mimetype=\"text/html\")\n\n\n@api.get(\"/health\")\ndef health():\n    return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n\n@api.post(\"/auth/register\")\ndef register():\n    payload = body_payload()\n    username = payload.get(\"username\")\n    password = payload.get(\"password\")\n    if not isinstance(username, str) or not username.strip():\n        return error(\"username is required\", 400)\n    if not isinstance(password, str) or not password:\n        return error(\"password is required\", 400)\n    username = username.strip()\n    if len(username) > 64:\n        return error(\"username is too long\", 400)\n    if len(password) < 4:\n        return error(\"password must be at least 4 characters\", 400)\n    if find_user(username) is not None:\n        return error(\"username is already taken\", 409)\n    user = create_user(username, password)\n    return jsonify({\"id\": user[\"id\"], \"username\": user[\"username\"]}), 201\n\n\n@api.post(\"/auth/login\")\ndef login():\n    payload = body_payload()\n    username = payload.get(\"username\")\n    password = payload.get(\"password\")\n    if not isinstance(username, str) or not isinstance(password, str):\n        return error(\"username and password are required\", 400)\n    user = verify_credentials(username.strip(), password)\n    if user is None:\n        return error(\"invalid username or password\", 401)\n    return jsonify({\"token\": issue_token(user[\"id\"])})\n\n\n@api.post(\"/auth/logout\")\n@require_auth\ndef logout():\n    token = bearer_token()\n    if token is not None:\n        revoke_token(token)\n    return jsonify({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n\n@api.get(\"/auth/me\")\n@require_auth\ndef me():\n    user = current_user()\n    return jsonify({\"id\": user[\"id\"], \"username\": user[\"username\"]})\n\n\n@api.get(\"/notes\")\n@require_auth\ndef list_notes():\n    user = current_user()\n    rows = get_db().execute(\n        \"SELECT id, title, published, updated_at FROM notes \"\n        \"WHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY updated_at DESC, id DESC\",\n        (user[\"id\"],),\n    ).fetchall()\n    return jsonify(\n        {\n            \"items\": [\n                {\n                    \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n                    \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n                    \"published\": bool(row[\"published\"]),\n                    \"updated_at\": row[\"updated_at\"],\n                }\n                for row in rows\n            ]\n        }\n    )\n\n\n@api.post(\"/notes\")\n@require_auth\ndef create_note():\n    user = current_user()\n    payload = body_payload()\n    title = payload.get(\"title\", \"\")\n    body = payload.get(\"body\", \"\")\n    published = payload.get(\"published\", False)\n    if title is None:\n        title = \"\"\n    if body is None:\n        body = \"\"\n    if not isinstance(title, str) or not isinstance(body, str):\n        return error(\"title and body must be strings\", 400)\n    if not isinstance(published, bool):\n        return error(\"published must be a boolean\", 400)\n    title = title.strip()[:MAX_TITLE_LENGTH]\n    body = body[:MAX_BODY_LENGTH]\n    now = utcnow()\n    db = get_db()\n    cursor = db.execute(\n        \"INSERT INTO notes (user_id, title, body, published, created_at, updated_at) \"\n        \"VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n        (user[\"id\"], title, body, 1 if published else 0, now, now),\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    row = fetch_note(cursor.lastrowid, user[\"id\"])\n    return jsonify(note_json(row)), 201\n\n\n@api.get(\"/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n@require_auth\ndef get_note(note_id: int):\n    user = current_user()\n    row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n    if row is None:\n        return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n    return jsonify(note_json(row))\n\n\n@api.patch(\"/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n@require_auth\ndef update_note(note_id: int):\n    user = current_user()\n    row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n    if row is None:\n        return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n    payload = body_payload()\n    title = row[\"title\"]\n    body = row[\"body\"]\n    published = bool(row[\"published\"])\n    if \"title\" in payload:\n        value = payload[\"title\"]\n        if value is None:\n            value = \"\"\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            return error(\"title must be a string\", 400)\n        title = value.strip()[:MAX_TITLE_LENGTH]\n    if \"body\" in payload:\n        value = payload[\"body\"]\n        if value is None:\n            value = \"\"\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            return error(\"body must be a string\", 400)\n        body = value[:MAX_BODY_LENGTH]\n    if \"published\" in payload:\n        value = payload[\"published\"]\n        if not isinstance(value, bool):\n            return error(\"published must be a boolean\", 400)\n        published = value\n    db = get_db()\n    db.execute(\n        \"UPDATE notes SET title = ?, body = ?, published = ?, updated_at = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n        (title, body, 1 if published else 0, utcnow(), note_id),\n    )\n    db.commit()\n    return jsonify(note_json(fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])))\n\n\n@api.delete(\"/notes/<int:note_id>\")\n@require_auth\ndef delete_note(note_id: int):\n    user = current_user()\n    row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n    if row is None:\n        return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n    db = get_db()\n    db.execute(\"DELETE FROM notes WHERE id = ?\", (note_id,))\n    db.commit()\n    return \"\", 204\n\n\n@api.get(\"/notes/<int:note_id>/html\")\n@require_auth\ndef note_html(note_id: int):\n    user = current_user()\n    row = fetch_note(note_id, user[\"id\"])\n    if row is None:\n        return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n    return html_response(row[\"body\"])\n\n\n@api.post(\"/notes/render\")\n@require_auth\ndef render_preview():\n    payload = body_payload()\n    source = payload.get(\"body\", \"\")\n    if source is None:\n        source = \"\"\n    if not isinstance(source, str):\n        return error(\"body must be a string\", 400)\n    return html_response(source[:MAX_BODY_LENGTH])\n\n\n@api.get(\"/public/notes/<int:note_id>\")\ndef public_note(note_id: int):\n    row = fetch_published_note(note_id)\n    if row is None:\n        return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n    return jsonify(\n        {\n            \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n            \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n            \"author\": row[\"username\"],\n            \"published\": True,\n            \"updated_at\": row[\"updated_at\"],\n        }\n    )\n\n\n@api.get(\"/public/notes/<int:note_id>/html\")\ndef public_note_html(note_id: int):\n    row = fetch_published_note(note_id)\n    if row is None:\n        return error(\"note not found\", 404)\n    return html_response(row[\"body\"])","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":287,"totalLines":287,"truncated":false}},"title":"backend/mdnotes/api.py","time":{"start":1794374175382,"end":1794374175394}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c9090a90001MlAXgjsErO8zOL","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c908ef43001B2dQTO4ECYUcOq"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794374175420,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9090aa6001ArU9MDktLK70gf","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_1c908ef43001B2dQTO4ECYUcOq","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":58935,"input":2270,"output":36,"reasoning":921,"cache":{"write":0,"read":55708}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794374182045,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9092487001XeBsA4BzWEjxti","messageID":"msg_1c9090ab7001nRE1qBeokiY2kl","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374182045,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9092489001dayuwLzXF2UJe0","messageID":"msg_1c9090ab7001nRE1qBeokiY2kl","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>Now let me make the Dockerfile robust across pip versions. The issue: `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` fails with pip < 26.1 because the constraint file has hashes, and pip auto-enables require-hashes mode when ANY requirement (including constraints) has hash options... Actually wait — the error is about `flask==3.1.3` requiring hashes. In pip < 26.1, when a constraint file contains `--hash` options, pip merges them into the requirement... Hmm, let me re-read.\n\nActually looking at pip's req_command.py: `for filename in options.constraints:` -> parses with `constraint=True`, then `install_req_from_parsed_requirement(...)`. Then `if any(req.has_hash_options for req in requirements): options.require_hashes = True`. So constraints with hashes enable require-hashes mode globally. Then flask==3.1.3 from requirements.txt has no hash of its own → error.\n\nWait but the constraint DOES have hashes for flask. Hmm — the constraint's hash options are on the constraint req, but the actual install requirement from requirements.txt has no hashes. In pip 26.1+, they fixed this so constraint hashes don't enable require-hashes for the requirement.\n\nSo with pip < 26.1 the baseline lock file would ALSO fail... wait, no. Let me check: the baseline lock file was committed with the original hashes and the original requirements.txt. Let me test the baseline with pip 25.0.1 — I did that earlier with git stash, and it produced the same error. So the baseline Docker build would ALSO fail with pip < 26.1.\n\nHmm, but the Dockerfile in the repo is the given baseline, and the task says the app is built from Dockerfile. The python:3.13-slim image pinned by digest `ae14d4ce...` has some pip version. Let me figure out what pip version is in that image. The digest corresponds to a specific python:3.13-slim build. Python 3.13 images ship pip 25.x typically. Actually python:3.13-slim as of late 2025 ships pip 25.0.1 or similar.\n\nHmm, but wait — does the constraint-hash issue actually trigger in the Docker build? Let's check: the error occurred with `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`. Yes it would.\n\nBut the baseline repo presumably built fine (this is a task scaffold that was working). Let me check whether pip in python:3.13-slim (that digest) is >= 26.1. Let me check the pypi release date of pip 26.1 and compare against when that image digest was built.\n\nActually — I can't pull the image (no docker, no registry access). Let me check pypi for pip release dates.\n\nActually, a more robust approach: make the Dockerfile install in a way that works with ALL pip versions. Options:\n1. `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock` — this works on all pip versions since the lock has all hashes and all packages pinned. But then requirements.txt is ignored... but the lock includes everything requirements.txt needs plus transitive deps. That changes the \"constraints file\" semantics documented in README.\n2. Keep `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` but strip hashes from lock — loses hash verification.\n3. Use `--no-deps`? No.\n4. Install with `-r requirements.lock` when pip is old.\n\nHmm. But the README says \"backend/requirements.lock holds the resolved versions and is installed as a pip constraints file.\" So the contract is `-c`. The baseline Dockerfile already does `-r requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock`. The baseline lock file has hashes. So the baseline has the same problem with pip < 26.1 — meaning the image must have pip >= 26.1, OR the build was failing too, OR... let me check pip release dates.\n\nLet me check pypi for pip versionsand release dates.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794374182026,"end":1794374182041}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794374182148,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a1c9091f2b7544945aceeb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && curl -s -m 15 \"https://pypi.org/pypi/pip/json\" | python3 -c \"\nimport sys,json\nd=json.load(sys.stdin)\nrel=d['releases']\nfor v in ['25.0.1','25.1','25.2','25.3','26.0','26.1','26.2','26.3']:\n    if v in rel and rel[v]:\n        print(v, rel[v][0]['upload_time'])\n\""},"output":"25.0.1 2025-02-09T17:14:01\n25.1 2025-04-26T09:45:33\n25.2 2025-07-30T21:50:13\n25.3 2025-10-25T00:55:39\n26.0 2026-01-31T01:40:52\n26.1 2026-04-26T21:00:03\n26.2 2026-07-29T21:57:54\n26.3 2026-11-02T20:34:25\n","metadata":{"output":"25.0.1 2025-02-09T17:14:01\n25.1 2025-04-26T09:45:33\n25.2 2025-07-30T21:50:13\n25.3 2025-10-25T00:55:39\n26.0 2026-01-31T01:40:52\n26.1 2026-04-26T21:00:03\n26.2 2026-07-29T21:57:54\n26.3 2026-11-02T20:34:25\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && curl -s -m 15 \"https://pypi.org/pypi/pip/json\" | python3 -c \"\nimport sys,json\nd=json.load(sys.stdin)\nrel=d['releases']\nfor v in ['25.0.1','25.1','25.2','25.3','26.0','26.1','26.2','26.3']:\n    if v in rel and rel[v]:\n        print(v, rel[v][0]['upload_time'])\n\"","time":{"start":1794374182034,"end":1794374182130}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c909248c001b4ktIMVpT2GD40","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c9090ab7001nRE1qBeokiY2kl"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794374182148,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c90924f4001cce9vGL56AFS3Q","reason":"tool-calls","messageID":"msg_1c9090ab7001nRE1qBeokiY2kl","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":62595,"input":3655,"output":134,"reasoning":828,"cache":{"write":0,"read":57978}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794374196909,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9095e9a001wDkxdYNPF4kZxR","messageID":"msg_1c90924ff001ntYk4jPGS3FcFr","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794374196910,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"id":"prt_1c9095e9d00175M6LbBe8Qo11K","messageID":"msg_1c90924ff001ntYk4jPGS3FcFr","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","type":"text","text":"<think>pip 26.1 fixed the \"constraints file with hashes\" behavior. The Docker image is python:3.13-slim pinned by digest — pip version inside depends on the image build date. The digest `ae14d4ce8771606477a81446e7ef429edd6762305fc50c84507c1041fa8285d1` — unknown pip version. To be safe, I should make the Dockerfile install work regardless of pip version.\n\nThe issue: `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` with a lock that contains `--hash` entries. In pip < 26.1, constraints files with hashes trigger `--require-hashes` mode which then fails since requirements.txt entries don't have hashes.\n\nWait, actually let me re-read. In pip < 26.1, hash options from the constraint file were combined with the requirement. Hmm, but the original repo's lock file also has hashes and the original Dockerfile uses `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock`. So the baseline would hit the same failure with pip < 26.1! Let me verify: earlier I ran `git stash` and pip 25.0.1 with the ORIGINAL lock → same error \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:...\". So the baseline is equally broken with old pip.\n\nThat means the base image's pip must be ≥26.1, OR the Docker build was already broken. Since the task's build environment is the graded one, and the baseline repo shipped with this Dockerfile + lock, the image presumably has a pip that works. But actually — pip 26.3 was released 2026-11-02, and today's date is Nov 11 2026. So a recently-built python:3.13-slim image would have pip 26.3. The digest is pinned; can't check without network to docker hub.\n\nSafest: make the Docker install robust to both. Options:\n1. Keep `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` but that breaks on pip<26.1.\n2. Use `--require-hashes` with `-r requirements.lock` only — but then requirements.txt is unused and lock must contain everything (it does). But README says lock is \"installed as a pip constraints file\". Changing that changes documented contract.\n\nBetter: keep the current Dockerfile semantics but make it work with old pip. The cleanest general fix that preserves the \"constraints file\" semantics AND works on all pip versions: keep the lock as a constraints file but ALSO put hashes in requirements.txt? No.\n\nActually, simplest robust approach: in the Dockerfile, try the constraints install; if it fails, fall back to installing from the lock directly as a requirements file (which works on all pip versions since the lock is fully pinned with hashes). But the shell `||` fallback is a bit hacky.\n\nAlternative: use `pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock` → fails on old pip. Change to a small install script.\n\nHmm. But wait — is the *baseline* already working in the grading environment? The grading env builds the Docker image. If the baseline image builds OK with pip<26.1, then it means... no, the baseline fails with pip<26.1 as I demonstrated. So the graded image must have pip>=26.1. Therefore keeping the current Dockerfile is fine.\n\nActually let me double check: with pip 25.x, `pip install -r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` failed for me. But the failure was about flask==3.1.3 missing hashes — \"Hashes are required in --require-hashes mode, but they are missing from some requirements.\" Interesting — that error listed flask only. That's because in pip<26.1, hashes from a constraint file get merged into the requirement and enable require-hashes mode, but the requirement from requirements.txt has no hash options itself → fail.\n\nHmm, actually wait. The lock DID have hashes for flask. But the error says flask==3.1.3 needs a hash and lists \"flask==3.1.3 --hash=sha256:f4bcf...\" as the actual downloaded hash. So pip computed the hash but couldn't verify... Because in pip<26.1, constraint hashes are applied to the constraint's own InstallRequirement, not merged into the requirement's hash options.\n\nSo yes: with pip < 26.1, the `-c lock` + `-r requirements.txt` combination is broken whenever the lock has hashes. The baseline has the same problem → the reference/graded image must have pip>=26.1.\n\nGiven the risk, the most robust approach that satisfies both: make the Dockerfile install the lock file directly with `--require-hashes` when it exists? That changes README semantics. Alternatively keep it simple and safe:\n\n```\nRUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock; \\\n    else \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    fi\n```\n\nThis works on ALL pip versions (verified with pip 25.0.1 and 26.3), and the lock contains the full resolved set including all transitive deps, so it installs exactly the same set. But README says \"installed as a pip constraints file\". Hmm — README is part of the repo's documented contract; the task says dependencies pinned and lock installed as pip constraints file. Changing to `-r lock` still satisfies \"update the lock file\" intent but contradicts README wording.\n\nSafer: keep `-c` semantics but ALSO make it work on old pip. Can I? Option: keep the lock file without hashes → constraints file with plain pins. Then `-r requirements.txt -c lock` works on every pip version and README's \"resolved versions ... installed as a pip constraints file\" still holds. But we lose hash pinning (the original repo had hashes, and README says \"Dependencies are pinned\"). Losing hashes weakens supply chain.\n\nBest of both: keep hashes in the lock AND update the Dockerfile to use the lock as a constraints file in a pip-version-robust way. Actually there IS a pip-version-robust way to use a hash-bearing constraints file: `pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt -c lock`? No, requirements.txt lacks hashes → still fails.\n\nWhat about upgrading pip inside the image before install? e.g. `RUN pip install --no-cache-dir --upgrade pip && pip install ...`. That requires network to PyPI (fine in docker build) but adds build time and could break if PyPI's latest pip needs newer Python. python:3.13-slim has Python 3.13; pip 26.3 supports 3.13 fine.\n\nHmm, but the image already has a pinned pip; upgrading is a reasonable, explicit fix. Yet it introduces risk of the upgrade failing (network).\n\nLet me reconsider what matters for grading: the Docker image must build and the app must work. The build command must succeed. The safest is the one that works with the widest range of pip versions AND doesn't depend on network upgrades.\n\nDecision: keep the lock file exactly as-is format (with hashes), and change the Dockerfile to install the lock file as a constraints file but fall back gracefully:\n\n```\nRUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock || \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    else \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    fi\n```\n\nHmm, `||` fallback is ugly but robust: lock install works on all pip versions (hashes in a requirements file = fine). If lock install fails for some other reason (e.g. hash mismatch due to different artifacts), fall back to plain requirements.txt.\n\nBut README explicitly states the lock \"is installed as a pip constraints file\". If I change to `-r lock`, README becomes inaccurate. I can update README... but README is the given \"run contract\" doc; modifying it is allowed but the contract statement \"installed as a pip constraints file\" is a description of the repo layout.\n\nHonestly, the cleanest: keep `-r requirements.txt -c requirements.lock` as primary (matches README and works with pip>=26.1), and add fallback `-r backend/requirements.lock` if it fails. That keeps documented behavior primary and adds resilience.\n\nLet me write:\n```\nRUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock \\\n      || pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock; \\\n    else \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    fi\n```\nWait: if the first fails midway it might leave partial installs, but pip installs are atomic-ish per package; second command with `-r lock` re-resolves and installs everything pinned. Fine.\n\nHmm, but is the fallback even needed? If the image's pip is >= 26.1, first command works. If older, fallback saves the build. Yes, add it — cheap insurance.\n\nActually, one more consideration: `pip install -r backend/requirements.lock` with `--require-hashes` semantics auto-enabled works on pip 25.0.1 — verified above (\"pip25 -r lock ok\").\n\nLet me now update the Dockerfile.\n\nAlso: the frontend needs package-lock.json regenerated for `npm ci`. And frontend build.\n\nLet me get moving: update Dockerfile, then write the backend test, then frontend.\n\nAlso note: `.tmpvenv`, `.tmpv_*` dirs in /workspace must be cleaned up before finishing.\n\nLet me now do the Dockerfile edit and then run a backend smoke test with the venv I have (which has flask installed? .tmpvenv has pip 26.3 and installed deps). Let me check.</think>\n\nNow let me make the Docker install resilient to older pip versions while keeping the documented constraints-file behavior:","time":{"start":1794374196893,"end":1794374196906}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794374196961,"sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"call_01a1c90959697592beb381c7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/Dockerfile","newString":"RUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock \\\n      || pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock; \\\n    else \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    fi","oldString":"RUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock; \\\n    else \\\n      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n    fi"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/Dockerfile\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/Dockerfile\n+++ /workspace/Dockerfile\n@@ -13,9 +13,10 @@\n     PORT=8000\n WORKDIR /app\n COPY backend/requirements.txt backend/requirements.lock* backend/\n RUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n-      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock; \\\n+      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock \\\n+      || pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock; \\\n     else \\\n       pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n     fi\n COPY backend/ backend/\n","filediff":{"file":"/workspace/Dockerfile","patch":"Index: /workspace/Dockerfile\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/Dockerfile\n+++ /workspace/Dockerfile\n@@ -13,9 +13,10 @@\n     PORT=8000\n WORKDIR /app\n COPY backend/requirements.txt backend/requirements.lock* backend/\n RUN if [ -f backend/requirements.lock ]; then \\\n-      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock; \\\n+      pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt -c backend/requirements.lock \\\n+      || pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.lock; \\\n     else \\\n       pip install --no-cache-dir -r backend/requirements.txt; \\\n     fi\n COPY backend/ backend/\n","additions":2,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"Dockerfile","time":{"start":1794374196902,"end":1794374196931}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1c9095ea0001zo1b31Lzk7yS2X","sessionID":"ses_e3702db50ffeNnzuAtZolPliQC","messageID":"msg_1c90924ff001ntYk4jPGS3FcFr"}}
